  "card": {
    "title": "Low contrast in code windows across the app, especially red/green diff highlights",
    "type": "feature",
    "scope": "# Summary\n\nCode windows across the app have too-low contrast, making code hard to read. It is especially bad where red/green diff highlights are applied, and gets worse when viewing the monitor from more than a couple of feet away. This is an accessibility issue.\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n1. Open any code window in the app (e.g. a diff/review view).\n2. View code, particularly lines with red/green added/removed diff highlights.\n3. Step back a few feet from the monitor.\n\nExpected: Code text is comfortably readable against both the base background and diff highlight backgrounds, including at a normal viewing distance.\nActual: Contrast is too low; text over red/green highlights is very hard to read, worse at distance.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Code text meets WCAG AA contrast (≥ 4.5:1) against the base code background\n- [ ] Code text over added/removed diff-line highlights meets WCAG AA contrast (≥ 4.5:1)\n- [ ] Added vs. removed diff highlights remain distinguishable and readable at typical viewing distance\n- [ ] Verified in both light and dark themes\n\n# Dev Hints\n\n* Review the code/diff theme colors app-wide; verify foreground text contrast against both the base background and the added/removed highlight backgrounds.\n* Use the existing `--tm-*` design tokens rather than hard-coded colors.",
